  "record_id": "005",
  "problem": "Channel additivity violations",
  "collection": "Original research investigation",
  "summary": "A discrete character-shift invariance is incorrectly extended to a continuous phase deformation, omitting derivatives of C_dep.",
  "review_status": "source_reviewed",
  "review_note": "No open interpretation blocker was recorded in the preceding source review. This is not a new independent mathematical verification.",
  "original": {
    "excerpt": "Choose balanced integer representatives of the three field coefficients \\(t_u,t_v,t_w\\), and let \\(q_T\\) be the corresponding vector in the real line-coordinate space. A continuous fixed-magnitude line-phase variation has\n\n$$\nC_{\\rm ind}(\\theta)\n=\n2\\sum_T\n\\operatorname{Re}\n\\left(\nA_Te^{i\\langle q_T,\\theta\\rangle}\n\\right).\n$$\n\nIf \\(C=1\\), then the coefficient field is physical and gives a global maximum of \\(C\\) on the entire phase torus. Hence\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\n\\sum_Tq_T\\,\\operatorname{Im}A_T=0,\n}\n\\tag{10.1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\n\\sum_T\n(\\operatorname{Re}A_T)\\,\nq_Tq_T^{\\mathsf T}\n\\succeq0.\n}\n\\tag{10.2}\n$$\n\nThese are the exact stationarity and Hessian conditions.",
    "context": "The total cubic objective is C=C_dep+C_ind. The proposed continuous line-phase variation multiplies coefficients by exp(i t theta_L), where t is a balanced integer representative of a finite-field element. The displayed equations differentiate only C_ind.",
    "locator": "round-18-response.md, lines 796–829"
  },
  "witness": {
    "explanation": "A discrete character-shift invariance is incorrectly extended to a continuous phase deformation, omitting derivatives of C_dep.",
    "evidence": "For odd D, set t=(D-1)/2. The same-line triple (t,t,1) sums to zero in F_D, so its cubic term belongs to C_dep. Its balanced integer sum is D, however, so the proposed continuous deformation multiplies that monomial by exp(iD theta_L), which is not constant. The gradient and Hessian of C_dep are therefore generally nonzero; equations using only C_ind are not the derivatives of the full objective. For D=5, the triple is (2,2,1).",
    "scope": "argument_invalid"
  },
  "archived_witness_excerpt": null,
